628 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 188 0 R/E(During active labour most people will be experiencing regular surges that require more of their attention. You will likely find movement, breathing and finding a rhythm with your surges will help you to ride each one. It\220s common for the birther to appear a bit more spaced out or in their own zone during this part and its the birth partners role to protect that space and try and leave them uninterrupted where possible.)/Pg 39 0 R/K[0]>>
endobj

629 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 188 0 R/E(This phase may start weeks, days or hours before baby is born. Signs may be feeling very emotional, mucus plug, nesting, wanting to stay close to home and changes in bowel movements. You may experience gentle surges that are regular for a while and then slow and stop again, this is very common and is all your body getting ready to birth your baby. During the time your cervix is beginning to move forwards, soften and shorten. It can be frustrating to have a long latent phase as it feels like things are beginning and then they disappear again but never forget that all of it is helping to bring your baby closer to being born. A long latent phase can mean that once the next phase starts up, everything starts to move a lot faster. Check out www.spinningbabies.com for positions and exercises to do during latent labour incase this would help things to progress. You can go about your life as usual as much as you want to during this phase.)/Pg 39 0 R/K[1]>>
endobj

630 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 188 0 R/E(By this point you are likely to be having regular surges that don\220t seem to be getting further apart or stopping. Changes to your environment or the people in it and stresses can still slow or stop labour at this stage though so be mindful of your environment. You may begin to think about your comfort measures such as getting in the shower or bath, massage and TENS machine. You may choose to be alone during this part in your own little bubble or have a partner, friend or family member with you. This may be helpful during the day if there are other children around or for providing food and drinks. Try to remain upright, forward and open throughout early labour but if you want to rest then do so. Care providers would measure this part as from the beginning of labour until around 4cm and a rule of thumb being until surges are around 3 or 4 in 10 minutes. )/Pg 39 0 R/K[2]>>
endobj

631 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 188 0 R/E(However, this is just a guide and if you begin to feel like you would very much like to get to your birth space, be seen by a midwife or receive a form of pain relief that isn\220t available to you alone at home, then this is probably the right time to call for a midwife to come to you or start making your way to your birth space.)/Pg 39 0 R/K[3]>>
endobj

632 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/P 188 0 R/T(Early Labour )/E(Early Labour )/Pg 39 0 R/K[4]>>
endobj

633 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/P 188 0 R/T(Active Labour)/E(Active Labour)/Pg 39 0 R/K[5]>>
endobj

634 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 188 0 R/E(The following is a general guide but all labours and births are different. Not everyone will be aware of all the stages or experience birth in the same way.)/Pg 39 0 R/K[6]>>
endobj

635 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/P 188 0 R/T(Latent Phase)/E(Latent Phase)/Pg 39 0 R/K[7]>>
endobj

636 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H1/P 188 0 R/T(stages of labour)/E(stages of labour)/Pg 39 0 R/K[8]>>
endobj

637 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 189 0 R/E(At some point after your cervix is 10cm dilated the foetal ejection reflex or \217urge to push\220 will kick in. The length of time between full dilation and this occurring varies from person to person. Sometimes without care provider patience, interventions can be suggested when they are not necessary. If all is well with yourself and baby then there\220s no need to do anything but wait. Many people call this phase the \217rest and be thankful\220 phase and it is great to just conserve energy and wait for that urge to kick in. Research suggests that the time between full dilation and baby being born is no different whether you wait for the foetal ejection reflex or are coached to push so save the energy and wait for your body to do it for you. As the birthing phase kicks in you may feel like you need a poo, perhaps you do! If there is poo in the way then as your baby comes down it will make its way out. Don\220t hold back! Your care providers will be very used to this and will be ready to clear it away respectfully. As everything in this area is so close together, the feeling of your baby about to be born can just feel a lot like needing a poo. The only way to know is to just go with it! At some point you will feel either a strong urge to bear down or that your body is doing it already and you couldn\220t stop it if you tried! Use your birth breathing to give you something to focus on and help it along. It\220s usual to make a loud involuntary \217mooing\220 noise at this point! Your babies head will usually be born first \(unless breech\) and then within the next few surges your baby\220s body will arrive. You\220ve done it and your baby is here with you!)/Pg 40 0 R/K[0]>>
endobj

638 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 189 0 R/E(The birth partner can be mindful of the environment, who\220s in it and any conversations that are taking place around the birther. You may wish to get in a birth pool at this stage or request additional comfort measures according to your wishes.)/Pg 40 0 R/K[1]>>
endobj

639 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 189 0 R/E(At some point just before baby makes its way into the world, there is a surge of adrenaline through your body. The purpose of this is to wake you up out of your birthing bubble to get you ready to care for baby and to initiate the birthing phase of labour. During this time you may feel nauseous, if you need to be sick, let it out. Others around you may notice a change in your behaviour as you become more aware of your surroundings and the sensations in your body. This is the time where many birthers begin to say things like \215I can\220t do this anymore\216, \215I need an epidural\216 or \215I need to go to hospital\216 if birthing at home. Birth partners and care providers can help the birther by providing reassurance and cheerleading. Recognising transition and knowing your baby will be here soon can help you to get through a sometimes difficult time.)/Pg 40 0 R/K[2]>>
endobj

640 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H1/P 189 0 R/T(Transition)/E(Transition)/Pg 40 0 R/K[3]>>
endobj

641 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H1/P 189 0 R/T(The Birthing Phase)/E(The Birthing Phase)/Pg 40 0 R/K[4]>>
endobj

642 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/Figure/P 190 0 R/Pg 41 0 R/A[1286 0 R 0]/K[0]>>
endobj

643 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 190 0 R/E(If the birther and baby are well then there is no need to interrupt the bonding process. Lots of time with baby on the birthers chest will help them both to produce oxytocin and reduce stress hormones. This aids bonding and helps with the placenta\220s arrival. Now is a great time for baby to have their first feed if you are planning to breastfeed, feeding soon after birth is associated with easier breastfeeding journeys. Things like weighing or measuring baby and getting baby dressed can wait until the birther is ready to have someone else hold baby while they get dressed perhaps.)/Pg 41 0 R/K[1]>>
endobj

644 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 190 0 R/E(\217Delayed\220 or optimal cord clamping should now be standard across all UK hospitals. At the time of baby\220s birth there is still up to a third of baby\220s blood inside the placenta, waiting to cut the cord until after this blood has been returned will improve baby\220s iron levels. Once the blood has left the placenta the cord will have gone from purple to white. The length of time this takes varies from person to person. You may wish to clamp \(or use a cord tie\) and cut the cord once this has happened or wait a while later, it\220s up to you!)/Pg 41 0 R/K[2]>>
endobj

645 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 190 0 R/E(After the birth of baby you are still technically in labour and so oxytocin and movement still help things along. Lots of people find sitting on the toilet helps to birth their placenta or just getting up and moving a bit. As previously mentioned skin to skin with baby can help boost oxytocin and cause the uterus to have the surges needed to detach and birth the placenta. You may be offered an injection of synthetic oxytocin to speed this process along. You can choose to have this or wait, there are pros and cons to both. Look into your options and decide which one you would like to go with. You can always opt to wait for the placenta to be born naturally and change your mind at any time. You\220re in charge here! )/Pg 41 0 R/K[3]>>
endobj

646 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H1/P 190 0 R/T(The Golden Hours)/E(The Golden Hours)/Pg 41 0 R/K[4]>>
endobj

647 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H1/P 190 0 R/T(Birthing the placenta)/E(Birthing the placenta)/Pg 41 0 R/K[5]>>
endobj

648 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H1/P 190 0 R/T(Clamping and cutting the cord)/E(Clamping and cutting the cord)/Pg 41 0 R/K[6]>>
endobj

649 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 191 0 R/E(Firstly, writing a birth plan is NOT a silly idea or an invitation to be disappointed. Anyone who thinks that way is not yet informed about how bloody important a birth plan is! A birth plan is not a prescription or a set of instructions, its just a clear presentation of what you want! It will help your care providers to know exactly what you want without asking you a million questions but most importantly it will help YOU and your birth partner/s to learn all the options available and know what you want!)/Pg 42 0 R/K[0]>>
endobj

650 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 191 0 R/E(Visual Birth Plan Icons. You can find these on www.pinterandmartin.com. These are a great way to have a first look at what all the options are. There are likely to be lots of them that you hadn\220t considered or have just never heard of before. Find out what they all mean if you\220re not sure, knowing what all the options are is a great way to start figuring out what you would like to choose!)/Pg 42 0 R/K[1]>>
endobj

651 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 191 0 R/E(You are looking to create AT LEAST 3 birth plans. Think about what changes could happen. Eg. Home birth to hospital, spontaneous to induction etc. You would then create birth plans for each scenario with plan A being your ideal birth and plan C being a caesarean birth. Someone planning a Labour ward birth may do a plan A for that ideal birth they are work- ing towards, a plan B for if they decide to have an induction or any augmentation \(waters being broken, the drip etc.\) and then a final plan C for caesarean. If plan A was a home birth or birth centre birth then there may be a plan B x2 for a change of birth place too!)/Pg 42 0 R/K[2]>>
endobj

652 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 191 0 R/E(Think about what you want! What\220s important to you? What birth have you always thought would be lovely? At this point it doesn\220t matter what type of birth you think you are limited to, for now you are just getting an idea of the type of birth you would love to have. The next steps will help you find out how you can make this happen or what alternatives there are.)/Pg 42 0 R/K[3]>>
endobj

653 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 191 0 R/E(What special circumstances do you need to consider? Find out everything you can about these- Places like Evidence Based Birth, Sara Wickham\220s website, the NICE guidelines, AIMS. These may be health issues of your own, pregnancy related issues, health issues for baby. Not all of these will affect your birth choices but now is the time to find out which ones do if you have any!)/Pg 42 0 R/K[4]>>
endobj

654 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 191 0 R/E(Find out others experiences. For example, If you would love a home birth and you have gestational diabetes, Join a FB group like Home Birth Support Group UK and look for others experiences with GD. This can be a great way of getting some insight into what other people have found useful when making their decisions and the different care provider opinions others have come across. This might show you that it\220s not as black and white as you may have been led to believe. No matter what it is always your choice.)/Pg 42 0 R/K[5]>>
endobj

655 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H1/P 191 0 R/T(birth planning)/E(birth planning)/Pg 42 0 R/K[6]>>
endobj

656 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H1/P 192 0 R/T(Talk through your options with knowledgable folks. Your instructor or the AIMS helpline would be a good start, they can tell you what they know, any relevant research and help you come up with a list of questions for you to ask your care providers as well as stuff to read up on.)/E(Talk through your options with knowledgable folks. Your instructor or the AIMS helpline would be a good start, they can tell you what they know, any relevant research and help you come up with a list of questions for you to ask your care providers as well as stuff to read up on.)/Pg 43 0 R/K[0]>>
endobj

657 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H1/P 192 0 R/T(Discuss with your care provider. This may be a midwife or a consultant, do not be disheartened if you don\220t hear what you would like to hear, you can still chat through your circumstances with a Professional Midwifery Advocate and they can talk you through if what you are after is REALLY out of the question or not.)/E(Discuss with your care provider. This may be a midwife or a consultant, do not be disheartened if you don\220t hear what you would like to hear, you can still chat through your circumstances with a Professional Midwifery Advocate and they can talk you through if what you are after is REALLY out of the question or not.)/Pg 43 0 R/K[1]>>
endobj

658 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H1/P 192 0 R/T(Consider what things are super important to you and you would not like to miss out on unless theres an emergency. This may be remaining upright throughout labour and for the birth, skin to skin after birth, avoiding \217the drip\220 or any other intervention or medication etc. How can you try to make sure that you can have these things? Can they be worked into all your birth plans? Eg. Remaining upright- even in the case of epidural use you can be assisted into various positions that support birth. Skin to skin after birth, can it be arranged that your birth partner holds baby on your chest for skin to skin EVEN after a Caesarean under general anaesthetic? Avoiding \217the drip\220, this may mean you opt for a caesarean if you would rather. There are so many possibilities we\220re not always offered, if you aren\220t sure how you can still incorporate certain things then talk to your instructor!)/E(Consider what things are super important to you and you would not like to miss out on unless theres an emergency. This may be remaining upright throughout labour and for the birth, skin to skin after birth, avoiding \217the drip\220 or any other intervention or medication etc. How can you try to make sure that you can have these things? Can they be worked into all your birth plans? Eg. Remaining upright- even in the case of epidural use you can be assisted into various positions that support birth. Skin to skin after birth, can it be arranged that your birth partner holds baby on your chest for skin to skin EVEN after a Caesarean under general anaesthetic? Avoiding \217the drip\220, this may mean you opt for a caesarean if you would rather. There are so many possibilities we\220re not always offered, if you aren\220t sure how you can still incorporate certain things then talk to your instructor!)/Pg 43 0 R/K[2]>>
endobj

659 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H1/P 192 0 R/T(Create the birth plans for real! Consider everything you\220ve learned about any factors concerning your pregnancy and chats with PMA or midwife etc and bring it all together to create your plan using whichever format suits you best, try to ensure it will be easily read by the care providers and explained by your birth partner. )/E(Create the birth plans for real! Consider everything you\220ve learned about any factors concerning your pregnancy and chats with PMA or midwife etc and bring it all together to create your plan using whichever format suits you best, try to ensure it will be easily read by the care providers and explained by your birth partner. )/Pg 43 0 R/K[3]>>
endobj

660 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H1/P 192 0 R/T(Create your plan methodically by first considering your background such as how you feel about birth, how many babies you\220ve had, any special medical or cultural requirements. )/E(Create your plan methodically by first considering your background such as how you feel about birth, how many babies you\220ve had, any special medical or cultural requirements. )/Pg 43 0 R/K[4]>>
endobj

661 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H1/P 192 0 R/T(Then move onto the things that you would like to include in your environment such as low light levels, quiet, who you would like present \(eg. Are you happy to have student midwives present? A doula?\). )/E(Then move onto the things that you would like to include in your environment such as low light levels, quiet, who you would like present \(eg. Are you happy to have student midwives present? A doula?\). )/Pg 43 0 R/K[5]>>
endobj

662 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H1/P 192 0 R/T(Next consider what you would like for each stage of labour, comfort measures you\220d like to have available, what monitoring or interventions you would like to choose or avoid. Make sure to include what you would like your midwives to do, whether you would prefer them in the background or if you would really like the reassurance of having them right next to you when they\220re in the room. Include how you would like baby to be born, whether you would like direction from a caregiver or not and if you would be happy to accept forceps etc if thought necessary.)/E(Next consider what you would like for each stage of labour, comfort measures you\220d like to have available, what monitoring or interventions you would like to choose or avoid. Make sure to include what you would like your midwives to do, whether you would prefer them in the background or if you would really like the reassurance of having them right next to you when they\220re in the room. Include how you would like baby to be born, whether you would like direction from a caregiver or not and if you would be happy to accept forceps etc if thought necessary.)/Pg 43 0 R/K[6]>>
endobj

663 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/Figure/P 193 0 R/Pg 44 0 R/A[1287 0 R 0]/K[0]>>
endobj

664 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/Figure/P 193 0 R/Pg 44 0 R/A[1288 0 R 0]/K[1]>>
endobj

665 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H1/P 193 0 R/T(Then consider what you would like to happen once baby is here, would you like to find out the sex yourself? )/E(Then consider what you would like to happen once baby is here, would you like to find out the sex yourself? )/Pg 44 0 R/K[2]>>
endobj

666 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H1/P 193 0 R/T(Who will cut the cord? When will it be clamped \(if at all\)? )/E(Who will cut the cord? When will it be clamped \(if at all\)? )/Pg 44 0 R/K[3]>>
endobj

667 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H1/P 193 0 R/T(What about vitamin K injection for baby? Let those reading know how you would like to feed your baby and what you would like to happen with baby when they are born, would you like uninterrupted skin to skin? )/E(What about vitamin K injection for baby? Let those reading know how you would like to feed your baby and what you would like to happen with baby when they are born, would you like uninterrupted skin to skin? )/Pg 44 0 R/K[4]>>
endobj

668 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H1/P 193 0 R/T(Would you like baby wiped down before you hold them? This is all up to you! Finally, how would you like to birth the placenta? What would you like to do with it after?)/E(Would you like baby wiped down before you hold them? This is all up to you! Finally, how would you like to birth the placenta? What would you like to do with it after?)/Pg 44 0 R/K[5]>>
endobj

669 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H1/P 193 0 R/T(Do this with your birth partner ideally so that they understand it all and know the reasons for your choices. At the very least, go through it in detail with them, they will then be able to help relay it to your care providers on the day.)/E(Do this with your birth partner ideally so that they understand it all and know the reasons for your choices. At the very least, go through it in detail with them, they will then be able to help relay it to your care providers on the day.)/Pg 44 0 R/K[6]>>
endobj

670 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H1/P 193 0 R/T(Change your birth plan any time you want, we often learn new things and make new choices. Finally, you can change your mind on the day if you want to!)/E(Change your birth plan any time you want, we often learn new things and make new choices. Finally, you can change your mind on the day if you want to!)/Pg 44 0 R/K[7]>>
endobj

671 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H1/P 193 0 R/T(Download the birth planning guide to access your free templates for both vaginal and caesarean births.)/E(Download the birth planning guide to access your free templates for both vaginal and caesarean births.)/Pg 44 0 R/K[8]>>
endobj

672 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/Span/K[9]/Pg 44 0 R/P 1063 0 R>>
